title The recipe that crossed -- the pure core builds for iOS, and the wrapper needed nothing
prompt Keaton handed the baton back with kg; the crux rule picked the wrapper door from movement five's own measured gap.
think The diagnosis ran smallest-first: a one-line Zig program against the simulator SDK isolated the wall to the compiler rather than the wrapper, and two findings opened it -- with a sysroot set the compiler PREPENDS it to -L paths, so the library path is spelled sysroot-relative (-L/usr/lib), and -fstrip retires the one undefined symbol, a legacy dyld call the std debug unwinder references and newer SDKs no longer export.
think The wrapper needed nothing at all: rye build forwarded -target, --sysroot, -L, and -fstrip untouched, so movement five's booked one-lap fix dissolves into a recipe -- and both pure-core witnesses crossed as written, the ring and the parity oracle, each answering file as a Mach-O 64-bit arm64 executable for the simulator, zero edits to any module.
obs The reading takes a measured addendum the same night it was born, which is what a living reading is for: the projection said the door was about one lap, and the truth was better -- the door was a recipe. The spawn inside a booted simulator is the honest next move and it parked honestly: CoreSimulator's cold start outran two bounded waits, so the run-on-iOS proof opens the next rung rather than padding this one.
obs The peer holds its %337 lap staged in our shared index, so this commit goes pathspec-limited -- the cleaner tool the earlier collisions taught -- and their staging stands exactly as they left it.
loom cross_build=2 target=aarch64-ios-simulator recipe_flags=4 module_edits=0 spawn=parked-cold-start
